DESCRIPTION >
    The append-only metered usage log written by the `@zanreal/medusa-usage`
    Tinybird sink. One row is one immutable statement of fact: this much of this
    meter was consumed by this subject at this instant.

    Nothing here is ever updated or deleted. A mistake is corrected by appending
    its reversal, an event with a negative `quantity`, exactly like a ledger, so
    that a number computed today can be recomputed from the same rows in a year.

    ## `key` is the identity of the event, and the engine collapses on it

    `key` is a SHA-256 over what the event means, derived by the plugin, so a
    retried request, a replayed queue message or a backfill run twice produce the
    identical key. The sink's contract is at most one row per key.

    ClickHouse has no primary key constraint, so that guarantee is built from two
    halves that agree with each other:

    - the engine is `ReplacingMergeTree` over a sorting key that ends in `key`,
      so a background merge collapses identical rows. Merges are eventual: two
      copies of one event can both be on disk for minutes or hours;
    - every endpoint therefore collapses again at read time, `GROUP BY key` with
      `argMax(..., version)`. That is what makes the guarantee hold the instant a
      duplicate lands, rather than whenever a merge happens to run.

    The two rules pick the same row, which is the property that matters: a merge
    can only ever remove a row the read path was already discarding, so an answer
    cannot change under it.

    ## `version` decides which copy wins, and picks the first one

    `version` is the negated ingestion timestamp, so the *largest* version, the
    row `ReplacingMergeTree` keeps and the row `argMax` picks, is the *earliest*
    ingested. That matches the built-in Postgres sink, where the append is
    `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` and the first write of a key is the one
    that survives.

    It only becomes observable when a caller reuses an explicit `idempotencyKey`
    across events with different facts, which is a caller bug in either sink. It
    is pinned rather than left to merge order so that the two sinks answer the
    same, and so that the answer does not depend on when a merge ran.

    Both `recorded_at` and `version` are assigned by Tinybird, not sent by the
    sink: one clock decides ingestion order, the way `now()` does in Postgres.
    A fleet of Medusa instances with skewed clocks cannot disagree about which
    copy of an event arrived first.

    ## The sorting key

    `meter, subject, occurred_at, key`, partitioned by month of `occurred_at`.

    - It ends in `key`, so the tuple is unique per event and the engine can
      collapse on it.
    - Its prefix is the invoice question: one meter, one subject, one half-open
      window of event time. That query reads a contiguous range and nothing else.
    - The meter-wide question skips `subject`, so it cannot use the sorting key
      past the first column. The monthly partition is what keeps it cheap: a
      billing window is a month, so it touches one or two partitions instead of
      the whole log.

    `occurred_at` is event time, the only column any endpoint filters on.
    `recorded_at` is when Tinybird accepted the row and is never a filter:
    filtering on it would let a late-arriving batch change an answer that had
    already been given.

    ## `properties` is canonical JSON, as one string

    Dimensions are stored as the canonical JSON text of the whole bag, keys
    sorted, no whitespace: `{"region":"eu","tier":2}`. An event with no
    properties stores the empty string.

    It is the same text the plugin hashes into the deduplication key, produced by
    the same function, so the bag that was keyed and the bag that was stored
    cannot drift apart. A filter compares a dimension with `JSONExtractRaw`,
    which returns that value's own canonical text, and that is what makes the
    filter typed: `tier = 2` and `tier = "2"` are different filters matching
    different events, exactly as they are under the Postgres sink's `jsonb`
    containment.

    A `Map(String, String)` would be the more idiomatic ClickHouse choice and was
    the first shape here. It was withdrawn because a map's contents turned out to
    depend on how the rows were loaded: appending the same NDJSON through the
    data sources API rather than the events API filled every row's map with the
    union of the keys in the file, giving events an empty-string dimension they
    never carried. A column whose value depends on which door the data came in is
    not one to keep a billing record in. A string is the same bytes down every
    path.

TOKEN "medusa_usage" APPEND

SCHEMA >
    `key` String `json:$.key`,
    `meter` LowCardinality(String) `json:$.meter`,
    `subject` String `json:$.subject`,
    `quantity` Int64 `json:$.quantity`,
    `occurred_at` DateTime64(3) `json:$.occurred_at`,
    `source` Nullable(String) `json:$.source`,
    `properties` String `json:$.properties`,
    `recorded_at` DateTime64(3) `json:$.recorded_at` DEFAULT now64(3),
    `version` Int64 `json:$.version` DEFAULT -toUnixTimestamp64Milli(recorded_at)

ENGINE "ReplacingMergeTree"
ENGINE_PARTITION_KEY "toYYYYMM(occurred_at)"
ENGINE_SORTING_KEY "meter, subject, occurred_at, key"
ENGINE_VER "version"

INDEXES >
    INDEX idx_usage_events_key key TYPE bloom_filter GRANULARITY 1
